; 41-cert.kurt

; Every step Kurt accepts is found by a search -- and then checked again by a
; small *kernel*, which doesn't search at all: it takes the step's
; *certificate* (which rule, which facts, which values for the variables of
; the rule) and checks that the rule, filled in, really gives the line. The
; comment Kurt prints after a line, `; by 3(4)`, is the short form of that
; certificate. `cert N` shows the long form for line `N`:

load prop
bool A, B
use A implies B
use A
B                   ; "by 12(13)": the rule of line 12, with the fact of line 13
cert 14

; Closing a block has a certificate too:

show A implies A
proof
    assume A
        A
qed
cert 23

; If the kernel ever rejected a step the search had found, that would be a
; bug in Kurt: the line fails with a `KernelError`, which no `expect` can catch.
;
; On the command line, `kurt FILE.kurt` also writes the certificates of a
; completely checked file to `FILE.kurtc`. The next time, Kurt reads them,
; hands each one to the kernel, and doesn't need to search -- but only if
; `FILE.kurt` hasn't changed, and a certificate that doesn't check is just
; ignored: the `.kurt` file is the only source of truth, the `.kurtc` only a
; hint. `kurt --deps FILE.kurt` shows the files it loads, and whether their
; certificates are up to date; `kurt --no-kurtc` neither reads nor writes them.
